WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.980
Do you have DesignaKnit?

2
00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:06.860
Did you know that you could use it
with your LK 150 machine,

3
00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:13.820
a Brother KX 350, or any knitting machine
that doesn't have working electronics?

4
00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:18.100
In this tutorial,
we'll discuss some of the possibilities.

5
00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:24.140
We are assuming that you're
familiar with the  DesignaKnit basics the purpose of this tutorial is

6
00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:30.700
to give you an overview of how you can use
it with non-patterning machines

7
00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:35.060
or non-electronic machines,
no matter what machine you're using.

8
00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.540
Standard Garment Styling and Original

9
00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:42.980
Pattern Drafting are a powerful
way to create garment shapes.

10
00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:46.340
The idea is to use the graphical interface

11
00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:50.420
to create shapes in the
dimensions that you want.

12
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:56.500
Now, there are many, many tutorials
online for creating shapes.

13
00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:02.500
Once you've created your shape,
you don't need electronics to knit it.

14
00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.130
You can print out your knitting

15
00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.570
instructions in the format
that works for you.

16
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:16.570
After confirming your gauge, click >File
>Print and select the format you want.

17
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:24.930
Garment text or garment notation are
the most common formats for use

18
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.960
in this case.
You can even use them for hand knitting.

19
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:41.020
Another option is to use the Interactive
Knitting feature in DesignaKnit.

20
00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:45.260
For this example,
I'll use my raglan sweater shapes

21
00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:51.620
and after confirming my gauge, I'll
select the Interactive Knitting Tool.

22
00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:56.570
Now, for manual machines,
this tool can be used in two ways.

23
00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:02.180
First, you can purchase the SSL USB cable.

24
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.540
This cable will allow Designaknit to sense

25
00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:10.890
the movement of your knitting carriage as
you pass the carriage back and stitch.

26
00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.380
DesignaKnit increments the graphic

27
00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:18.980
display and instructs you what
to do while you're knitting.

28
00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:23.860
Now you don't have the cable,
you can still use Interactive Knitting

29
00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:30.820
simply manually increment the graphic
display using the arrows in the software

30
00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:37.610
or the arrow keys on your keyboard
so you could knit along a few rows

31
00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:44.520
and then up arrow, up arrow, up arrow
to the row that you're on manually.

32
00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:51.380
Either way, you follow the images
in the display in Interactive Knitting

33
00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:57.720
to increase, decrease and do whatever
else you need to shape your knitting.

34
00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.860
Now, you can also use DesignaKnit

35
00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:06.780
for stitch patterning on these
manual and non-electronic machines.

36
00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:09.180
Even if you have a manual machine,

37
00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:15.180
you use the same interactive Knitting
interface to manually select the needles.

38
00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:21.100
For example,
I've opened a Fairisle stitch pattern.

39
00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:23.500
You can follow along the stitch pattern

40
00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:27.820
in the lower section of the screen
to select your needles.

41
00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:31.300
To work your Fairisle manually,

42
00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:38.760
you can use the cable and magnet or
manually advance the rows with the arrows.

43
00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.020
Now, this may be a better example

44
00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:07.610
using tuck. On a manual machine,
you can select the needles to tuck

45
00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:12.660
according to the lower section
of Interactive Knitting.

46
00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:15.940
You'll just follow the diagram row by row,

47
00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:20.980
selecting your needles
and setting your carriage to tuck.

48
00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:27.420
In this example
again, you could use the cable and let

49
00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:34.620
DesignaKnit record what row you're
on or just increment the rows manually.

50
00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:40.540
Now finally, you can combine all of this
and integrate the stitch pattern

51
00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:45.660
with the shaping pieces and let
interactive knitting give you both shaping

52
00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:52.440
instructions and stitch patterning
instructions at the same time.

53
00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:04.980
Increase left one.

54
00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.140
Increase right one.

55
00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.820
I hope I piqued your curiosity about using

56
00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.260
DesignaKnit with your
non electronic machine.

57
00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.420
This wasn't intended as
a DAK tutorial in any way.

58
00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:22.880
Only an introduction as to what is
possible, no matter what machine you use.
